WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:02.530
Lately, we've been hearing that&nbsp;
unemployment is at a record low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:05.770
which is a good thing for the economy.&nbsp;
But I just got done looking deep into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:09.310
unemployment for this video we made. And my&nbsp;
big takeaway is unemployment is actually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:12.790
like, not super well understood. I think&nbsp;
most people think of unemployment as, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.770
the number of people who don't work. That's like&nbsp;
the rate that they give, like 3% or whatever. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:22.150
it's actually slightly more nuanced than that.&nbsp;
It's not everyone in the economy who can't find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:27.730
a job. It's actually the number of people who&nbsp;
want a job, who are like out looking for a job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:33.190
who can't find a job. There are a lot of jobs&nbsp;
in the economy that aren't counted because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:38.110
don't, like, pay money. So like people who care&nbsp;
for other people, like parents or something, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.550
don't count in the unemployment figure. It's only&nbsp;
people who are out there looking for jobs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:47.410
make money. And what it doesn't capture is people&nbsp;
who are overqualified for their jobs, like someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:52.450
who studied like to be a pharmacist but can't&nbsp;
find a job in being a pharmacist, and so they,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.930
like, teach tennis lessons or something. They're&nbsp;
technically employed, but they're underemployed.

